hello everyone in this video we'll talk  about  natural killer t cell or nkt cell  which is a mixed character of t  cell and natural killer cell they are  kind of a hybrid  now indeed these cells were actually  named in a transgenic mouse to describe  the t cell subpopulation which has a  characteristic symbol of  nk cells from that time this colloquial  term  was used as a nomenclature for these  cell types  let us look at the properties of nkt  cells the surface markers  and what the function of nkt cells in  this video  so nkt cells have alpha beta  t cell receptor which is similar to a t  cell  it has nk 1.1 receptor which is  characteristic of a  nk cells perforin and granzyme  containing  vesicles can be found in the nkt cells  it also have fc receptor  which binds to the fc region of  antibodies  and are very crucial for uptaking  oxinized pathogens  just like t cells they also secrete  cytokines  so this is a comprehensive overview of  their features which are similar to t  cell  or nk cells now let me tell you that  nk cells differ from other kind of t  cell  though they have a t cell receptor which  is alpha  beta type but these alpha beta t cell  receptors have very less diversity  in terms of recognition and they don't  recognize  mhc class 2 rather than they recognize  limited a variety of lipid or  glycolipids present  on cd1 molecules which is a non  classical mhc like molecule so this is  the major difference between a nkt cell  and a th  cell because th cell always recognize  pathogens or pathogen-derived and  antigens on top of class 2 mhc whereas  nkt cell  determines only few or limited varieties  of lipid and glycolipid  which is presented to them on top of cd1  molecules  this is the major difference of that now  nkt cell seems to be essential for  various aspects of immunity especially  in mouse models or transgenic mouse  models where  nkt cell production was abrocated in  those mouse model the risk of autoimmune  disease  has increased so obviously we can  understand the pres  involvement of these cell types in  autoimmune responses or to prevent the  autoimmune responses  nkt cells secrete varieties of cytokines  which leads to diverse function so let  us look at some of them  so it secretes il2 il4 interferon gamma  inter tnf alpha il6 etc  il4 and interferon gamma  are polarizing cytokines interferon  gamma  and il4 helps in production of  th1 subpopulation of t cells or th2  subpopulation of t  cells so indeed nkt cells can direct the  differentiation of t helper cell into  specific sub population  moreover tnf alpha and il6 are  characteristics  inflammatory cytokine so they take part  in  inflammation as well now nkt cells have  fc receptors  so they can really  go and engulf opsinized  viruses or any kind of oxonized pathogen  with their  fc receptor so here you can see  they can go and bind to the fc region of  oxinized  virus and literally endocytose or  phagocytose  that virus and that is how they can  neutralize the virus  moreover these cytotoxic cells eliminate  targets  infected with intracellular pathogens  such as bacteria  and viruses also they are  critical against tumor cell  immunity so the immune immune response  against  tumor cell is orchestrated by the nkt  cells  and especially when there is a  temperature associated trauma  these nk t cells are found to be  activated  still what is the exact mechanism it's  still unknown  but these are the overall functions of  nkt cells  in terms of immunity now let us talk  about  how these nkt cells are developed the  nkt cells  are developed in the thymus just like  the t helper cells  inside the thymus the imagio t cells  come inside by high endothelial venule  they move into the subcapsular region  and in the cortex and corticomedulary  junction they interact  with thymocytes which give them  essential training in terms of  antigen determination now the double  positive stage has  both cd8 and cd4 core receptors  it's a stage of t cell d